{{nihongo|'''''Sake kasu'''''|酒粕|}} or '''sake lees''' are the pressed [[Lees (fermentation)|lees]] left from the production of [[sake]] (Japanese [[rice wine]]). It is a white paste used in cooking.<ref name=":0">{{cite web|title=With sake rice, nothing goes to waste|url=https://www.japantimes.co.jp/life/2011/01/28/food/with-sake-rice-nothing-goes-to-waste/#.Xpc9tMgzZPZ|last1=Itoh|first1=Makiko|date=January 28, 2011|website=The Japanese Times|access-date=May 21, 2020}}</ref> Its taste is fruity and similar to sake.<ref>{{Cite web|title=Sake Kasu Zanmai: What is Sake Kasu?|url=https://kyotofoodie.com/what-is-sake-kasu/|date=2009-03-10|website=Kyoto Foodie: Where and what to eat in Kyoto|language=en-US|access-date=2020-05-21}}</ref> A [[by-product]] of Japanese sake production, it typically contains 8% alcohol, has high nutritional value, and might have health benefits.<ref name=":5">{{Cite journal|last1=Mikami|first1=Nana|last2=Tsukada|first2=Yoshiro|last3=Pelpolage|first3=Samanthi Wathsala|last4=Han|first4=Kyu-Ho|last5=Fukushima|first5=Michihiro|last6=Shimada|first6=Kenichiro|date=2020-02-01|title=Effects of Sake lees (Sake-kasu) supplementation on the quality characteristics of fermented dry sausages|url= |journal=Heliyon|language=en|volume=6|issue=2|article-number=e03379|doi=10.1016/j.heliyon.2020.e03379|doi-access=free |pmid=32123761|pmc=7036523|bibcode=2020Heliy...603379M |issn=2405-8440}}</ref><ref name=":1">{{Cite web|title=Sake-Kasu: Amazing By-product of Sake {{!}} SAKETIMES - Your Sake Source|url=https://en.sake-times.com/learn/sake-kasu|date=2016-07-19|website=SAKETIMES|language=en|access-date=2020-05-21}}</ref> 

Sake kasu is used as a [[Marination|marinade]] for Japanese dishes based on fish, vegetables, and meat, and contributes an [[umami]] flavor to the dish.<ref name=":0" /> Sake kasu is also found in cosmetics and skincare products.<ref name=":9">{{Cite web|title=Homemade Japanese Beauty Treatment With "Sake Kasu"|url=https://trendandchaos.com/japanese-beauty-treatment-sake-kasu/|date=2020-01-01|website=Trend & Chaos|language=en-US|access-date=2020-05-21}}</ref> Sake kasu is considered a part of the Japanese [[Mottainai|"no waste"]] culture since the waste of the sake production is used in various ways.<ref name=":2">{{Cite web|title=Sake Kasu: Flower of the Garbage|url=https://www.edibleboston.com/blog/2018/6/11/sake-kasu-flower-of-the-garbage|website=Edible Boston|language=en-US|access-date=2020-05-21}}</ref> [[Mirin]], a type of sweet Japanese sake, can also produce kasu called mirin kasu.<ref name=":1" /> Similar to sake kasu, mirin kasu can also be used as a healthy food ingredient.<ref name=":10">{{Cite web|title=What is Mirin Kasu (sweet sake lees)? {{!}} We Love Japanese Food|url=https://welovejapanesefood.com/mirin-kasu/|date=2017-09-18|website=welovejapanesefood.com|language=en-US|access-date=2020-05-21}}</ref>
